<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Views"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="300" d:DesignHeight="596"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.LightingModeRailView"
             x:DataType="vm:LightingTabViewModel">
  <!--
    The Lighting tab's rail. IT IS A PROPERTIES PANEL (issue #128), and that is the whole change:
    mockup 2f drew a scrolled list of fourteen modes filling most of the column with a fixed
    parameter footer pinned under it, and the user's own words were "instead of a list occupying
    most of the space, a dropdown ... and show only relevant options" and "I want to know that the
    controls I'm seeing are part of the mode I've selected".

    So, in order down the rail: the mode, as a ComboBox whose rows are the very markup the list rows
    were; then ONLY the properties that mode actually has — its colours, the picker that edits them,
    its speed, its direction — each with one line under it saying what it means IN THAT MODE.

    IT IS STILL THE KEY INSPECTOR RAIL'S FRAME, DELIBERATELY. Same `inspectorRail` surface and
    hairline, same `inspectorSection` blocks separated by hairlines rather than by gaps. The two
    rails are the same object in two tabs, and a second rail vocabulary would say they were not.

    IT SETS NO WIDTH OF ITS OWN, and that is issue #124's point: a GridSplitter resizes a COLUMN, so
    a local Width here would outrank the column the rail lives in and the two would disagree the
    moment the seam beside it was dragged.

    WHAT THE MODE DOES *NOT* DECIDE is anything about painting, wherever paint is possible at all.
    The per-key colours belong to the layer rather than to the effect running over them (2f: "the
    colors are still on file"), so the picker is on screen even where the mode has no swatch of its
    own — it is the colour a paint gesture uses — and so are the paint line and the zones, which
    live over on the board side. The exception is the pair of modes that write NOTHING, Off and
    Pitch Black (Parameters.AcceptsPaint, specs/07-lighting.md §2.2): there the picker goes too,
    because a colour picked in them reaches no file and no LED.

    NOTHING IS PINNED ANY MORE. The footer existed because a mode picked at the top of a 600px list
    must not push its own speed off the bottom; there is no list, and a properties panel whose
    fields scroll with their labels is the shape the Settings tab already uses.

    MONO IS THE FILE'S VOICE HERE TOO. `#57C4D8` and `Speed 6 / 9` are values the led file carries;
    the mode names, the parameter summaries, the hints and the section labels are the app talking
    about them. (The section labels are `sectionLabel`, which is mono by role — the design's own
    10/600 caption over a group of controls — and are authored uppercase because Avalonia has no
    text-transform.)
  -->
  <Border Classes="inspectorRail">
    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*">

      <!-- ===== The mode ======================================================================
           2f's heading over the one control that decides what the rest of this rail contains, so it
           is the pinned header rather than the first thing in the scroll.

           A COMBOBOX WITH THE LIST'S OWN ROWS. The item template is what each `Button.modeOption`
           drew: the mark, the name, and the parameter summary underneath — so the closed control
           shows the selected mode exactly as its row did, and the drop-down is the same fourteen
           rows a column-high list used to spend the rail on. `SelectableListRow` is the container
           theme Themes/ControlThemes/Fields.axaml writes for exactly this ("a view that wants the
           redesign's drop-down rows sets ItemContainerTheme to it").

           ONE-WAY, WITH THE SELECTION CHANGE RUNNING THE COMMAND. `SelectedMode` is the panel's own
           property with a private setter — the same shape the layer switch and the section strip
           have — so the control reads it and `OnModeSelectionChanged` writes through
           SelectModeCommand.

           Nothing here knows a mode name or a parameter: membership is LightingAvailability's, the
           caption LightingModeCaptions', the summary Core's LightingModeParameters, and the line
           under the box LightingHintCatalog's. -->
      <Border Grid.Row="0" Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:LightingTabViewModel.ModeRailCaption}"
                     Classes="cardTitle"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" />

          <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding Modes}"
                    SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedModeOption, Mode=OneWay}"
                    SelectionChanged="OnModeSelectionChanged"
                    PlaceholderText="{Binding ModeCaption}"
                    ItemContainerTheme="{StaticResource SelectableListRow}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                    HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch">
            <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingModeViewModel">
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
                  <!-- The mode's mark, which "draws the motion rather than naming it twice"
                       (docs/design/handoff.md). Two Icons in one cell because thirteen of the
                       sixteen marks are outlines and three are solids, and those are different
                       properties on an Icon: the converter answers only the half the mode is drawn
                       in, so the other draws nothing. Stroking a ring *and* filling it would turn
                       it into a disc, which is why they are never both answered. -->
                  <Panel Grid.Column="0"
                         Width="{DynamicResource IconSize}"
                         Height="{DynamicResource IconSize}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <controls:Icon Classes="modeMark"
                                   Data="{Binding Mode, Converter={StaticResource LightingModeMark}, ConverterParameter=Stroke}" />
                    <controls:Icon Classes="modeMark filled"
                                   Data="{Binding Mode, Converter={StaticResource LightingModeMark}, ConverterParameter=Fill}" />
                  </Panel>

                  <!-- A Grid column rather than a StackPanel child: a StackPanel measures with
                       infinite width, so a long summary would run off the rail instead of
                       ellipsing inside it.

                       The summary carries `meta muted` beside its own marker class. It used to take
                       both from `Button.modeOption :is(TextBlock).modeSummary` in Styles/Editor.axaml,
                       which cannot reach a ComboBoxItem — and the roles it set are exactly the two
                       generic ones, so naming them is the same result with no new style. -->
                  <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Spacing="1" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Summary}"
                               Classes="modeSummary meta muted"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Grid>
              </DataTemplate>
            </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
          </ComboBox>

          <TextBlock Text="{Binding ModeHint}" Classes="meta muted" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The selected mode's properties, and nothing else ==============================
           Every block below is gated on Core's answer for the selected mode
           (LightingModeParameters), so what is on screen IS what the mode has — the picker
           included, on AcceptsPaint rather than on a swatch, because what it edits is the layer's
           paint colour rather than a parameter of the effect.

           They scroll as one column. The rail is the full height of the editor now, so the whole of
           a mode's properties fits without scrolling in every mode but the widest — but the picker
           alone is ~460px tall, so the ScrollViewer is what makes the rail survive a short window
           rather than a decoration. -->
      <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
        <StackPanel>

          <!-- ===== Colour ====================================================================
               The effect and base swatches. Clicking one points the picker at it (the view model's
               command) AND scrolls the picker into view (the view's own handler) — one gesture,
               because a swatch that re-targeted an editor sitting below the fold would look dead.
               The picker is never hidden, though: this only ever moves the rail, never discloses.

               EACH CARRIES ITS OWN LINE, and that pair is the question this issue was raised over:
               "I don't understand what is the difference between Effect and Base color in reactive
               mode". In Reactive the base is the colour every key rests at between strikes and the
               effect is the colour a key flashes when struck (specs/07-lighting.md §2.2 writes the
               first as the `[mono]` line and the second as the `[reactive]` line) — so the rail
               says so, in place, rather than leaving two same-shaped rows to be told apart by name.

               The effect swatch is also RENAMED where it is not effect colour at all: §2.2 writes
               no effect-colour line for Freestyle, Breathe or Frozen Wave, whose bodies are per-key
               lines, so there it reads "Paint Color" (LightingColorSlotViewModel.EffectCaptionFor). -->
          <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding Parameters.AcceptsAnyColor}">
            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.ColorLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

              <StackPanel Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding EffectColor.IsVisible}">
                <controls:AccessibleButton Classes="colorSlot"
                                           Classes.selected="{Binding EffectColor.IsSelected}"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                           HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
                                           Command="{Binding SelectColorSlotCommand}"
                                           CommandParameter="{Binding EffectColor}"
                                           Click="OnColorSlotClicked">
                  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                    <!-- The one place a colour legitimately arrives as data rather than as a token:
                         it is the LED colour the file carries, converted by HexColorToBrush. -->
                    <Border Grid.Column="0"
                            Classes="colorSwatchFill"
                            Background="{Binding EffectColor.ColorHex, Converter={StaticResource HexColorToBrush}}" />
                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                               Text="{Binding EffectColor.Caption}"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                               Text="{Binding EffectColor.ColorHex}"
                               Classes="monoValue"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  </Grid>
                </controls:AccessibleButton>

                <TextBlock Text="{Binding EffectColor.Hint}" Classes="meta muted" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
              </StackPanel>

              <StackPanel Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding BaseColor.IsVisible}">
                <controls:AccessibleButton Classes="colorSlot"
                                           Classes.selected="{Binding BaseColor.IsSelected}"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                           HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
                                           Command="{Binding SelectColorSlotCommand}"
                                           CommandParameter="{Binding BaseColor}"
                                           Click="OnColorSlotClicked">
                  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                    <Border Grid.Column="0"
                            Classes="colorSwatchFill"
                            Background="{Binding BaseColor.ColorHex, Converter={StaticResource HexColorToBrush}}" />
                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                               Text="{Binding BaseColor.Caption}"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                               Text="{Binding BaseColor.ColorHex}"
                               Classes="monoValue"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  </Grid>
                </controls:AccessibleButton>

                <TextBlock Text="{Binding BaseColor.Hint}" Classes="meta muted" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
              </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- ===== Speed =====================================================================
               Nine bars, not a slider: the file's speed is nine discrete values (§2.1) and a slider
               would suggest a continuum. The readout is mono because `6` and `9` are what `[spd6]`
               carries. -->
          <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding Parameters.AcceptsSpeed}">
            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                           Text="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.SpeedLabel}"
                           Classes="sectionLabel"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                           Text="{Binding SpeedControl.Readout}"
                           Classes="monoValue"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </Grid>

              <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding SpeedControl.Segments}">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                  </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingSpeedSegmentViewModel">
                    <!-- No content at all: `SpeedBar` is a fixed 10x18 shape with no padding, and
                         the level is read off how many of them are filled. -->
                    <controls:AccessibleButton Classes="speedBar"
                                               Classes.filled="{Binding IsFilled}"
                                               Command="{Binding $parent[views:LightingModeRailView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).SetSpeedCommand}"
                                               CommandParameter="{Binding Speed}" />
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              </ItemsControl>

              <TextBlock Text="{Binding SpeedHint}" Classes="meta muted" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- ===== Direction =================================================================
               ONLY THE ARROWS THE MODE ACCEPTS, AND THE BLOCK IS ABSENT WITHOUT ONE — and that
               REVERSES mockup 2f, in as many words: "Directions a mode can't use stay in place,
               struck through — the row never changes shape as you move down the list."

               THE JUSTIFICATION WENT WITH THE LIST. 2f's rule was about a fourteen-row column the
               user scrubs: a parameter row that shrank and grew under the pointer as the selection
               moved would be worse than a struck arrow. There is no column to scrub — the mode is a
               dropdown, one commit at a time — so the cost the exception was bought at no longer
               exists, and what is left is four dead controls under a heading on eleven of the
               fourteen modes, in a rail whose whole point is now "the controls you see belong to
               the mode you picked".

               So the app's own law applies again, the one 2f was overriding: features a device (or
               here a mode) lacks are not rendered at all rather than disabled. `IsAvailable` is
               still Core's answer and is still what SelectDirectionCommand validates against; the
               view merely stops drawing the false ones. The `.unavailable` state stays in the
               DirectionSegment control theme, unused here.

               The arrow is geometry, never one of `← → ↑ ↓`: neither embedded IBM Plex family is
               guaranteed to carry them, and the icon law is "geometry only". The name behind it
               ("Left", or Rebound's "Horizontal") is the tooltip, so relabelling a mode's axes is
               not lost to a picture. -->
          <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding Parameters.AcceptsDirection}">
            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.DirectionLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

              <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Directions}">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" />
                  </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingDirectionViewModel">
                    <!-- The Icon carries no class: Styles/Editor.axaml colours it from the BUTTON's
                         own classes, so the view writes the state once. -->
                    <controls:AccessibleButton Classes="directionSegment"
                                               Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
                                               IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable}"
                                               ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}"
                                               Command="{Binding $parent[views:LightingModeRailView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).SelectDirectionCommand}"
                                               CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                      <controls:Icon Data="{Binding Direction, Converter={StaticResource LightingDirectionMark}}" />
                    </controls:AccessibleButton>
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              </ItemsControl>

              <TextBlock Text="{Binding DirectionHint}" Classes="meta muted" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- ===== The colour picker =========================================================
               ALWAYS OPEN (issue #128). It used to be disclosed from a swatch and drawn IN PLACE OF
               the mode list, which was the only place in the rail tall enough to hold it. There is
               no list to displace now, so the disclosure earned nothing and a toggle that only ever
               hid a control the user had just asked for is one click for nothing.

               It is the very same ColorPickerViewModel the tab holds, so the ten premixed swatches
               and the twelve `cust_color_N` slots are the ones the Settings tab's strip shares.

               IT IS ON SCREEN IN EVERY MODE THAT HAS SOMETHING FOR IT TO WRITE — Parameters.
               AcceptsAnyColor, i.e. the mode carries an effect swatch, a base swatch, or per-key
               colours. The line under it says which of them a pick lands on.

               THE GATE MOVED OFF AcceptsPaint WITH ISSUE #135, and it had to. It used to be on
               screen in every mode whose paint reaches the file, including the ones with no swatch
               of their own, because a colour picked here painted the keys selected on the board.
               Now that only a per-key mode has a selection to paint, that reading leaves modes like
               Wave — no swatch, no selectable keys — showing a colour wheel that writes NOTHING.
               AcceptsAnyColor is exactly "this mode has a colour of some kind", and every per-key
               mode satisfies it (Core derives the effect swatch from WritesEffectColor OR
               HasPerKeyColors), so nothing that can still be painted lost its picker.

               IT WAS ALREADY ABSENT IN THE TWO THAT WRITE NOTHING. specs/07-lighting.md §2.2 gives
               Off and Pitch Black no file body at all, which is why LightingSectionSerializer
               returns before its first line: there is no effect for a colour to show through and no
               line for one to land on. Drawing a colour wheel under "The backlight is off for this
               layer, and nothing is written to the file for it" was the defect a captured frame
               caught — the rail promised a write the file cannot hold, in the one control that had
               been left ungated. AcceptsAnyColor is false in both, so they stay excluded.

               IT IS LAST, AND A FRAME IS WHY. It sat directly under the colour swatches, where it
               reads best — and it is ~460px tall, so on a 680px window it pushed Speed and
               Direction off the bottom of the rail in every mode that has them. Wave's captured
               frame showed a rail whose only visible properties were the mode and a colour wheel.
               The compact properties therefore come first and the picker takes the space that is
               left; clicking a swatch scrolls it back into view (OnColorSlotClicked), so choosing a
               target still puts its editor on screen the way the disclosure used to. -->
          <Border x:Name="PickerSection"
                  Classes="inspectorSection"
                  IsVisible="{Binding Parameters.AcceptsAnyColor}">
            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static views:LightingModeRailView.PickerLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

              <controls:ColorPickerView DataContext="{Binding Picker}" />

              <TextBlock Text="{Binding PickerHint}" Classes="meta muted" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

        </StackPanel>
      </ScrollViewer>
    </Grid>
  </Border>
</UserControl>
